        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-48042",
        ":question/problem": "![](images/8e/8eb53506d9e01d09fe3e072a2c6ed1ade05e1cc185693bdd66b34bdf75797ad9.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. \n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)$ is finite\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)$ is finite\n- $f(x)$  has a discontinuity at $x=2$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is false. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function contains a break exactly at $x=2$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper."
      },
