        ":question/problem": "![](images/80/8086893e4587e68f8257b75f2070cf622fa141199d6bd8960f7c4fd8e7bcb349.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is **not** defined at $x=0$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)$ exists\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph we can see that no value has been assigned to $f(x)$ at $x=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x) & =3, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function is not even defined at $x=0.$ The graph contains a break at $x=0.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."
