        ":question/worked-solution": "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is false. From the graph we can see that no value has been assigned to $f(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function is not even defined at $x=1.$ The graph contains a break at $x=1.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."
