        ":question/problem": "![](images/fa/fa15192923f9fd7370818fcb6b05b24b3bb6901dfbf90dd53539d71fd0f8b0bf.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=f(2)$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$ and $f(2)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=1=f(2).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of $f(x)$ can be drawn without taking our pencil off of the paper in the neighborhood of $x=2.$"
